    "shop_smart":  [
                       "\u003cstrong\u003ePork shoulder is the cheapest honest cut in the case.\u003c/strong\u003e It is fatty on purpose, and that fat is what makes this sauce taste rich without a drop of cream. Buy it whole and dice it yourself instead of paying for pre-cut.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eHoisin does the heavy lifting here.\u003c/strong\u003e The traditional sauce leans on two fermented bean pastes most stores do not carry, and hoisin plus soy gets you to the same salty-sweet place from one jar that lasts many batches.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eCucumber and carrot are the cheapest things in this cart.\u003c/strong\u003e Do not skip them thinking they are garnish. The raw crunch against the hot sauce is doing real work.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eCook the noodles the day you eat them if you can.\u003c/strong\u003e Store the sauce on its own and boil a portion fresh, and the texture holds up far better than a pre-mixed container."
                   ],
    "make_it":  [
                    "Weigh your empty mixing pot and write the number down for portioning later.",
                    "Dice the pork shoulder small, roughly pea-sized, so it eats like a sauce rather than a stew.",
                    "Heat the vegetable oil in a heavy pot with the bay leaves, ground cinnamon, and black pepper and let them sizzle a minute to flavor the oil, then cook the pork over medium-high heat until it browns and renders its fat. Fish out the bay leaves once the oil smells fragrant.",
                    "Add the garlic, ginger, and the white parts of the green onions and cook until fragrant.",
                    "Stir in the hoisin sauce, soy sauce, and sugar and let them fry in the fat for a minute, which is what takes the raw edge off.",
                    "Pour in the chicken broth, season with the salt, drop the heat to low, and simmer uncovered until the sauce is thick and glossy and the pork is tender.",
                    "Boil the lo mein noodles to just tender, drain, and rinse briefly so they do not clump.",
                    "Cut the cucumber and carrots into matchsticks, then serve the sauce over the noodles with the raw vegetables and the green onion tops piled on."
                ],
